1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,662
What if, every time you said one tiny word, a dollar appeared in your pocket?

2
00:00:04,982 --> 00:00:06,247
The word is no.

3
00:00:06,567 --> 00:00:10,793
No to the snooze button. No to the upgrade. No to the thing you didn't even want.

4
00:00:11,113 --> 00:00:13,087
Sounds like the easiest money on Earth.

5
00:00:13,407 --> 00:00:18,806
But by the end of this video, you'll see why that single dollar is worth way more than a dollar.

6
00:00:19,126 --> 00:00:21,645
And why one no almost cost you everything.

7
00:00:22,445 --> 00:00:25,289
It starts on a Monday. Your alarm goes off at seven.

8
00:00:25,609 --> 00:00:28,942
Your thumb is already on the snooze button, and you mumble the word.

9
00:00:29,262 --> 00:00:29,552
No.

10
00:00:29,872 --> 00:00:32,647
Ding. A crisp one dollar bill drops onto your pillow.

11
00:00:32,967 --> 00:00:36,101
You stare at it. You say it again, just to test. No.

12
00:00:36,421 --> 00:00:37,443
Ding. Another one.

13
00:00:38,243 --> 00:00:40,170
So here are the rules of your new life.

14
00:00:40,490 --> 00:00:44,879
Every time you say no and mean it, you get one dollar. Real money, in your account.

15
00:00:45,199 --> 00:00:48,438
It has to be a real no to something you were about to do or buy.

16
00:00:48,758 --> 00:00:53,147
You can't stand in front of a mirror shouting no all day. You tried. Nothing happened.

17
00:00:53,467 --> 00:00:55,243
And the money only counts if you keep it.

18
00:00:56,043 --> 00:00:59,886
First stop, the coffee shop. The barista already knows your order.

19
00:01:00,206 --> 00:01:03,445
The usual? Large caramel latte with an extra shot?

20
00:01:03,765 --> 00:01:08,107
You open your mouth, and out comes a no. Just a regular black coffee, please.

21
00:01:08,427 --> 00:01:11,980
Ding. Plus one dollar. And you saved three more on the drink itself.

22
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:15,144
The barista looks at you like you just insulted her mother.

23
00:01:15,944 --> 00:01:21,424
Then your phone buzzes. It's Max, your best friend and the worst financial advisor alive.

24
00:01:21,744 --> 00:01:27,317
Bro. Limited drop. Sneakers. Two hundred and twenty dollars. They sell out in ten minutes.

25
00:01:27,637 --> 00:01:30,005
Your heart says yes. Your thumb types no.

26
00:01:30,325 --> 00:01:34,308
Ding. And somewhere, two hundred and twenty dollars stay exactly where they are.

27
00:01:34,628 --> 00:01:36,915
Max sends eleven crying emojis.

28
00:01:37,715 --> 00:01:40,803
By the end of day one, you've said no seven times.

29
00:01:41,123 --> 00:01:46,951
No to the snooze. No to the latte. No to the sneakers. No to a free trial you would have forgotten to cancel.

30
00:01:47,271 --> 00:01:49,860
No to delivery, when there was food in the fridge.

31
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:54,128
Seven dollars. It doesn't sound like a superpower. It sounds like a coupon.

32
00:01:54,448 --> 00:01:58,081
But you're not looking at the seven dollars. You're looking at what you didn't spend.

33
00:01:58,401 --> 00:02:02,511
Two hundred and sixty dollars, in one day, just because you paused.

34
00:02:03,311 --> 00:02:05,552
By Friday, you've become a professional.

35
00:02:05,872 --> 00:02:11,073
No to the extra charger. No to the gym you never go to. No to the third streaming service.

36
00:02:11,393 --> 00:02:14,807
You start saying no to things before anyone even offers them.

37
00:02:15,127 --> 00:02:17,634
The cashier asks if you want a bag. No. Ding.

38
00:02:17,954 --> 00:02:24,479
Your bank app starts to look different. For the first time, the number at the end of the week is bigger than the number at the start.

39
00:02:25,279 --> 00:02:26,893
Of course, Max finds out.

40
00:02:27,213 --> 00:02:31,741
He shows up at your door with a notebook and a terrible plan.

41
00:02:32,061 --> 00:02:34,801
Okay, I ask you stupid questions all day, you say no, we split the money. Do you want a pet crocodile?

42
00:02:35,121 --> 00:02:35,341
No. Nothing happens. Not a single dollar.

43
00:02:35,661 --> 00:02:37,902
Because the power only pays when the no actually costs you something. When there was a real yes on the table.

44
00:02:38,222 --> 00:02:40,591
Max looks at the notebook, sighs, and asks if you want to get pizza. You almost say yes. Almost.

45
00:02:41,391 --> 00:02:45,419
One month in, you check the score.

46
00:02:45,739 --> 00:02:50,256
The magic dollars add up to two hundred and three. Nice, but not life changing.

47
00:02:50,576 --> 00:02:52,468
The money you didn't spend is a different story. Over one thousand dollars, still sitting in your account.

48
00:02:52,788 --> 00:02:58,024
That's the secret nobody puts on a thumbnail. The dollar was the reward. The real money was everything you didn't buy.

49
00:02:58,344 --> 00:03:02,930
And you start to wonder. What happens if this keeps going for years?

50
00:03:03,730 --> 00:03:08,641
And that's when things get weird.

51
00:03:08,961 --> 00:03:14,441
Your grandma calls. Sunday dinner, sweetheart? I made your favourite pie.

52
00:03:14,761 --> 00:03:21,866
And before you can think, the word slips out. No.

53
00:03:22,186 --> 00:03:27,028
Ding.

54
00:03:27,348 --> 00:03:31,109
For the first time, the dollar feels heavy.

55
00:03:31,429 --> 00:03:33,751
Because some things you say no to don't come back.

56
00:03:34,551 --> 00:03:39,311
So you sit in your kitchen and you realise the power was never about the dollar.

57
00:03:39,631 --> 00:03:43,103
The power was the pause. The tiny second between the urge and the purchase.

58
00:03:43,423 --> 00:03:49,402
That second is where your money lives.

59
00:03:49,722 --> 00:03:55,179
And the trick is not to say no to everything. It's to say no to things, so you can say yes to people.

60
00:03:55,499 --> 00:03:58,099
You call Grandma back. Yes. Yes to the pie. No dollar. Totally worth it.

61
00:03:58,899 --> 00:04:02,754
And saying no is hard for a reason. The world is designed to get a yes out of you.

62
00:04:03,074 --> 00:04:07,776
Countdown timers. Limited drops. Free trials that quietly turn into monthly payments.

63
00:04:08,096 --> 00:04:10,917
Buy now, pay later buttons that make two hundred dollars look like four small, friendly numbers.

64
00:04:11,237 --> 00:04:13,733
None of that is illegal. It just works best on people who don't pause.

65
00:04:14,053 --> 00:04:17,548
Your new superpower simply gives you one second to notice the trick.

66
00:04:18,348 --> 00:04:20,379
Now let's leave the fantasy and do the real math. Because this is where it gets crazy.

67
00:04:20,699 --> 00:04:23,497
Forget the magic dollar. What if you simply moved one dollar a day, every day, into an index fund?

68
00:04:23,817 --> 00:04:31,561
An S and P five hundred index fund owns a small slice of about five hundred of the biggest companies in America.

69
00:04:31,881 --> 00:04:34,970
No stock picking. No genius required. You just keep buying, month after month.

70
00:04:35,290 --> 00:04:42,592
And we're going to use real prices, starting in January twenty ten.

71
00:04:43,392 --> 00:04:45,064
Here's why investing changes everything. It's called compounding.

72
00:04:45,384 --> 00:04:49,424
Your dollar earns a little. Then that little bit starts earning too.

73
00:04:49,744 --> 00:04:54,179
It's like a snowball rolling down a hill. Small and slow at the top. Huge at the bottom.

74
00:04:54,499 --> 00:04:58,017
The hill is time. And the longer the hill, the bigger the snowball.

75
00:04:58,817 --> 00:05:02,103
Here's one dollar a day. About thirty dollars a month. Less than most phone plans.

76
00:05:02,423 --> 00:05:07,403
The first years look like nothing. A few hundred dollars. Boring.

77
00:05:07,723 --> 00:05:10,150
Then the crashes come. Twenty twenty. Twenty twenty two. Your account drops, and you keep buying anyway.

78
00:05:10,470 --> 00:05:16,530
By September twenty twenty six, you've put in six thousand one hundred and fourteen dollars.

79
00:05:16,850 --> 00:05:19,439
And it's worth about nineteen thousand eight hundred.

80
00:05:19,759 --> 00:05:21,083
From one dollar a day.

81
00:05:21,883 --> 00:05:26,887
Now imagine you're an average person who says no five times a day.

82
00:05:27,207 --> 00:05:33,349
Five dollars a day becomes thirty thousand five hundred and sixty nine dollars put in.

83
00:05:33,669 --> 00:05:36,943
And it grows to about ninety nine thousand dollars.

84
00:05:37,263 --> 00:05:39,097
Ten no's a day? Almost two hundred thousand.

85
00:05:39,417 --> 00:05:43,144
Not from a lottery ticket. Not from a crypto tip. From the word no.

86
00:05:43,944 --> 00:05:48,297
But what if you only discover your power later? Say, in January twenty sixteen?

87
00:05:48,617 --> 00:05:54,086
Five dollars a day from twenty sixteen means about nineteen thousand six hundred put in.

88
00:05:54,406 --> 00:05:56,832
And it's worth roughly forty three thousand today.

89
00:05:57,152 --> 00:06:01,471
Less than the early starter, because the hill was shorter. But still more than double.

90
00:06:01,791 --> 00:06:05,588
The best time to start was years ago. The second best time is your next no.

91
00:06:06,388 --> 00:06:08,199
But here's the part nobody tells you.

92
00:06:08,519 --> 00:06:11,561
The small no's are nice. The big no's change your life.

93
00:06:11,881 --> 00:06:19,532
Saying no to one new phone every two years. No to a car that's nicer than you need. No to a bigger apartment just because your friend got one.

94
00:06:19,852 --> 00:06:23,009
One big no can be worth a thousand small ones.

95
00:06:23,329 --> 00:06:30,702
If you said no to just one hundred dollars of stuff every month since twenty ten, you'd have put in twenty thousand one hundred dollars.

96
00:06:31,022 --> 00:06:33,913
And it would be worth about sixty five thousand two hundred.

97
00:06:34,713 --> 00:06:36,501
And here's another way to see it.

98
00:06:36,821 --> 00:06:40,896
In the US, the average worker earns about thirty eight dollars an hour.

99
00:06:41,216 --> 00:06:45,569
So those two hundred and twenty dollar sneakers? That's almost six hours of your life.

100
00:06:45,889 --> 00:06:49,338
When you say no, you're not just keeping money. You're keeping hours.

101
00:06:50,138 --> 00:06:53,679
So how do you get the power without the magic? Three simple tricks.

102
00:06:53,999 --> 00:06:59,049
One. The twenty four hour rule. Anything that isn't food or bills waits one day in your cart. Most of it, you'll forget.

103
00:06:59,369 --> 00:07:01,772
Two. Make the no automatic. Set a small transfer to savings on payday, before you can spend it.

104
00:07:02,092 --> 00:07:08,118
Three. Every month, cancel one thing you pay for but don't use. That's a no that keeps paying, every single month.

105
00:07:08,438 --> 00:07:11,747
None of this feels heroic. That's exactly why it works.

106
00:07:12,547 --> 00:07:17,678
Now, the honest part. This is not financial advice.

107
00:07:17,998 --> 00:07:24,419
These are real S and P five hundred prices, but the past doesn't promise the future.

108
00:07:24,739 --> 00:07:28,024
Markets can drop and stay down for years.

109
00:07:28,344 --> 00:07:30,179
Build an emergency fund first, pay off expensive debt, and only invest money you won't need soon.

110
00:07:30,979 --> 00:07:35,890
So, no magic dollar is coming. But the pause is real, and you already have it.

111
00:07:36,210 --> 00:07:42,688
Next time your thumb hovers over buy now, wait one second and ask: is this a thing, or a person?

112
00:07:43,008 --> 00:07:46,270
What's the one thing you'd say no to this week? Tell me in the comments.

113
00:07:46,590 --> 00:07:48,285
And I'll see you in the other timeline.

